From: Kervin Pursoty <kpursoty@proton.me>
Subject: clocksource: timer-econet-en751221: fix IRQ ack for one-shot operation

The interrupt handler acks the compare register by copying the current
count back to compare:

  iowrite32(ioread32(reg_count(cpu)), reg_compare(cpu));

The hardware fires an interrupt whenever count >= compare.  By the time
this MMIO write reaches the peripheral, the free-running counter has
advanced past the written value, so the pending condition (count >=
compare) re-asserts on the same bus cycle.  The result is an unintended
tight interrupt storm on CPU0 that starves all other IRQs.

The EcoNet GPL vendor kernel (arch/mips/econet/time2.c, function
cputmr_timer_ack()) acknowledges the HPT interrupt by writing the
*next* deadline to CMR:

  expirelo[vpe] += cycles_per_jiffy;
  regWrite32(cputmr_cmr[vpe], expirelo[vpe]);

Writing a value in the future (count < compare) clears the pending
condition.  For the one-shot case the correct equivalent is to write
U32_MAX, which is unreachable by the 32-bit counter before
set_next_event() is called to program the next real deadline.  This is
consistent with how cevt_dev_init() initialises the register.

Signed-off-by: Kervin Pursoty <kpursoty@proton.me>
--- a/drivers/clocksource/timer-econet-en751221.c
+++ b/drivers/clocksource/timer-econet-en751221.c
@@ -85,7 +85,9 @@
 	if (!cevt_is_pending(cpu))
 		return IRQ_NONE;

-	iowrite32(ioread32(reg_count(cpu)), reg_compare(cpu));
+	/* Ack: write a future value (U32_MAX) so count < compare, clearing the
+	 * pending condition.  set_next_event() will program the real deadline. */
+	iowrite32(U32_MAX, reg_compare(cpu));
 	dev->event_handler(dev);
 	return IRQ_HANDLED;
 }
